module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Pi
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.FaithfullyFlat.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ClosedSubspaceIdealQuasicoherent
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.SmallEtaleAffineRefinement
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.SmallEtaleAffineSectionBaseChange
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ZeroSchemeMaximality

/-!
# Closed subspaces from quasi-coherent sheaves of ideals

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`.  Stacks 03MB
asserts that sending a closed subspace `Z ⊆ X` to the kernel `𝓘_Z ⊆ 𝒪_X` of the structure map of
its inclusion is an inclusion-reversing bijection onto the quasi-coherent sheaves of ideals of
`𝒪_X` on the small étale site.  **This file proves the surjectivity of that correspondence, and
with it the bijection.**

What was missing was that a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals is *cartesian on affine charts*: that
for a morphism of affine étale charts `V ⟶ U` the sections over `V` are the base change of the
sections over `U`.  Granted that, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space`'s
`exists_definingIdealSheaf_eq_of_isCartesianOnAffineCharts` already produced the closed subspace.
For an arbitrary `𝒪_X`-module the cartesian condition is the forward implication of Descent,
Proposition 03DX(2) and is not available here; **for a sheaf of ideals it is proved below**, and
the asymmetry is genuine.  Injectivity of the comparison was already unconditional, coming from
the inclusion into `𝒪_X` together with flatness of the restriction maps
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.injective_sectionsBaseChange_submodule_unit`), so only surjectivity —
generation of the sections over the smaller chart by the restricted sections — was at stake, and
that is an equality of two *ideals* of the ring of functions on the smaller chart.  Faithfully
flat descent for ideals needs nothing beyond flatness, whereas producing a module over the base
out of local module data is the content of Stacks 023T.

Two inputs are composed.  Quasi-coherence supplies, over every affine étale chart `U`, a covering
family over whose affine members the sections of the sheaf of ideals *are* the base change of the
sections over `U`: that is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.SmallEtale`'s
`exists_hasSectionBaseChangeOn_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`, which is unconditional.  Given a second
affine chart `V ⟶ U`, that family is pulled back to the slice at `V` and refined by a finite
family of affine charts which is flat and jointly surjective in schemes, so that the product of
their rings of functions is faithfully flat over the ring of `V`.  Over each member of the
refinement the ideal generated by the sections over `U` and the ideal of sections over `V` have
the same extension, and `Ideal.eq_of_le_of_forall_map_le` identifies them.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedSubspaceQuasicoherentIdealOrderIso` is the order isomorphism of
  Stacks 03MB, together with the kernel formula that pins it down to the canonical rule.

## Main results

* `Ideal.eq_of_le_of_forall_map_le`: **faithfully flat descent for a pair of nested ideals.**  Two
  ideals of a commutative ring, one inside the other, which generate the same ideal in each member
  of a finite family of algebras whose product is faithfully flat, are equal.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.surjective_sectionsBaseChange_of_chartIdeal_le_map` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartIdeal_le_map_of_hasSectionBaseChangeOn`: the two translations
  between generation of the ideal of sections and the base change comparison.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.surjective_sectionsBaseChange_submodule_unit_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`
  and `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCartesianOnAffineCharts_submodule_unit_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`:
  **a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of `𝒪_X` is cartesian on affine charts**, unconditionally.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealSheaf_eq_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`: **Stacks 03MB,
  surjectivity** — a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of `𝒪_X` is the defining ideal sheaf of a
  closed subspace of `X`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.toDual_definingIdealSheaf_bijective'`: **the bijection of Stacks
  03MB.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`: the same closed
  subspace read chart by chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_zeroSchemeSubfunctor_eq_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`: the zero
  scheme of a section of an `𝒪_X`-module is the closed subspace cut out by the evaluation ideal
  of the section, as soon as that ideal is a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals.

## Implementation notes

The descent lemma is stated for a finite family of algebras rather than for a single faithfully
flat algebra, because that is the shape a covering family of an affine chart comes in; the proof
splits the unit of the product into the units of the factors and needs no tensor-product
identification beyond right exactness.

The ring of sections of the structure sheaf over a chart carries two spellings,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartRing` and the global functions on the underlying scheme.  They are
definitionally equal but instance search is syntactic, so the algebra structures of the
refinement are bound twice with the same value, following
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/SmallEtaleAffineSectionBaseChange.lean`.

Three statements elsewhere in this tree are made obsolete by this file and are *not* edited here.
The module documentation of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ClosedSubspaceIdealQuasicoherent.lean` says that the
surjectivity half of Descent 03DX(2) for sheaves of ideals "is **not** available in this tree",
that of `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroScheme.lean` that "the library has no
quasi-coherent ideals on an algebraic space at all", and that of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeMaximality.lean` that the surjectivity half of
Stacks 03MB "is missing".  All three were true when written.

